<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Range — reading it from a graph (JSXGraph)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The graph of \(y=f(x)\) is shown below. A <strong>filled</strong> dot means that \(y\)-value is <em>included</em>; an <strong>open</strong> dot means it is <em>excluded</em>.</p>
<div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="Graph of a straight line segment y = f(x) rising from a lower end point to an upper end point, each marked with a filled or an open dot.">
[[jsxgraph width="320px" height="280px"]]
(function() {
  var p = {#p#};
  var q = {#q#};
  var bottomClosed = {#bottomClosed#};
  var topClosed = {#topClosed#};
  var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [-5, q + 2, 5, p - 2],
    axis: true, grid: true,
    defaultAxes: {
      x: {ticks: {ticksDistance: 1, insertTicks: false, minorTicks: 0}},
      y: {ticks: {ticksDistance: 1, insertTicks: false, minorTicks: 0}}
    },
    showCopyright: false, showNavigation: false
  });
  var f = board.jc.snippet('{#fx#}', true, 'x', true);
  board.create('functiongraph', [f, -4, 4], {strokeColor: '#1565c0', strokeWidth: 2});
  board.create('point', [-4, p], {
    name: '', size: 4, fixed: true,
    fillColor: bottomClosed ? '#1565c0' : '#ffffff',
    strokeColor: '#1565c0', strokeWidth: 2
  });
  board.create('point', [4, q], {
    name: '', size: 4, fixed: true,
    fillColor: topClosed ? '#1565c0' : '#ffffff',
    strokeColor: '#1565c0', strokeWidth: 2
  });
})();
[[/jsxgraph]]
</div>
<p>State the range of \(f\) as a compound inequality in \(y\).</p>
<div>[[input:ans_lo]] [[validation:ans_lo]] [[input:ans_lo_type]] [[validation:ans_lo_type]] \(y\) [[input:ans_hi_type]] [[validation:ans_hi_type]] [[input:ans_hi]] [[validation:ans_hi]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt_lo]] [[feedback:prt_hi]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>The <strong>range</strong> of a function is the set of its output values, so on a graph we read it from the <strong>vertical</strong> extent of the curve: how far down and how far up it reaches along the \(y\)-axis. The lowest \(y\)-value reached is \(y={@p@}\) ({@bottomdotdesc@}) and the highest is \(y={@q@}\) ({@topdotdesc@}).</p>
<p>So the range is given by \({@lo_ineq@}\) and \({@hi_ineq@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2026063000</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text><![CDATA[p : rand_with_prohib(-6,-2,[]);
w : rand_with_prohib(4,8,[]);
q : p+w;                                /* p < q on every seed, so the swap diagnostic never equals the answer */
bottomClosed : is(rand(2)=1);
topClosed : is(rand(2)=1);
fx : (q-p)/8*x+((p+q)/2);              /* a simple line from (-4,p) to (4,q), decorative */
/* dropdown option lists: [literal value, is-correct, plain-text label]. LaTeX does not render in a dropdown, so labels are Unicode. */
lo_le : "leq"; lo_lt : "lt";
hi_le : "leq"; hi_lt : "lt";
loopts : [[lo_le, bottomClosed, "≤"], [lo_lt, not bottomClosed, "<"]];
hiopts : [[hi_le, topClosed, "≤"], [hi_lt, not topClosed, "<"]];
ta_lo : if bottomClosed then lo_le else lo_lt;
ta_hi : if topClosed then hi_le else hi_lt;
/* display inequalities for the worked solution and the note */
lo_ineq : if bottomClosed then p<=y else p<y;
hi_ineq : if topClosed then y<=q else y<q;
bottomdotdesc : if bottomClosed then "a filled dot, so it is included" else "an open dot, so it is excluded";
topdotdesc : if topClosed then "a filled dot, so it is included" else "an open dot, so it is excluded";]]></text>
    </questionvariables>
